Discuss it with me before you implement anything 

You’re fighting the browser’s pan/scroll gesture recognizer, and the current hook is trying to “win” after the browser has already decided “this is a scroll”.

The fundamental flaw (why scroll keeps hijacking)

On mobile, scrolling is not “just another event you can prevent later”. The browser decides very early (often before your pointermove threshold or your 200ms timer fires) whether the gesture is a pan. Once it commits to panning, your drag is basically toast.

Two key issues in your current approach:

1. You never opt the card out of native panning
e.preventDefault() on pointerdown does not reliably disable scrolling on touch devices unless the element’s CSS touch-action disallows panning (or you’re using non-passive touchmove prevention, which is messy and still imperfect on iOS).
✅ The real fix is: set touch-action: none on draggable cards (or at least while a pointer is down on them).


2. Timer uses a React synthetic event asynchronously (secondary bug)
You call startDrag(e) inside setTimeout. That e is a React PointerEvent (synthetic). Using it asynchronously is unsafe and can yield stale values. You mostly get away with it because you also start by movement threshold, but it’s still a bug.




---

What to change in the UI (required)

On the draggable card wrapper add touch-action: none

If you’re using Tailwind, that’s touch-none:

<div
  key={def.id}
  className={`relative transition-all duration-300 rounded-md ${stateClasses} touch-none`}
  onPointerDown={(e) => isOwned && !inDeck ? onPointerDown(e, def) : undefined}
  onContextMenu={(e) => e.preventDefault()}
/>

On the scroll container, explicitly allow vertical panning

Tailwind: touch-pan-y (or CSS touch-action: pan-y):

<div className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-4 pt-1.5 pb-48 touch-pan-y">

This combo is the “correct” modern solution:

Starting a gesture on a card won’t pan-scroll the page/container.

Starting a gesture on empty space will scroll naturally.



